// Original practice, not past-paper transcription. Page = new 15-page PDF counter.
export const divineEthicsPractice=[];
function q(module,id,page,difficulty,prompt,answer,why,...distractors){
  divineEthicsPractice.push({module,id,page,difficulty,prompt,answer,why,distractors});
}
q('planning','plan-versus-organization',4,2,'A student defines priorities and drafts a study schedule, but has not begun following it. Which distinction best describes this?','Planning has occurred; organization as implementation remains to be done','Printed page 59 distinguishes preparing the plan from implementing it. A schedule alone does not establish execution.',
 ['Organization is complete because the schedule looks orderly','Visual order does not establish implementation in the definition used here.'],['Planning begins only after all tasks are finished','Planning prepares the action rather than following its completion.'],['Planning and organization are defined as identical activities','The passage explicitly distinguishes preparation from implementation.']);
q('planning','daily-bank',4,2,'What does the expiring daily deposit of 86,400 units represent in the planning chapter?','The seconds available in a day, which cannot be carried forward unused','The analogy treats daily time as a finite opportunity. The number corresponds to 24 × 60 × 60 seconds.',
 ['The number of virtues a person must acquire','The number refers to time, not a catalog of virtues.'],['A reward that can be saved unchanged for the next day','The disappearing unused balance is essential to the analogy.'],['A requirement to perform 86,400 separate acts daily','The passage does not prescribe a count of acts.']);
q('planning','time-categories',5,2,'Which allocation matches the three time categories actually named in the source?','Worship and duties; work and provisions; permitted rest and leisure','The page includes all three dimensions and relates their proportions to needs and priorities.',
 ['Study, paid work and financial investment only','This omits worship and permitted rest, both explicitly named.'],['Worship, more worship and avoidance of all leisure','The source allows and allocates time to lawful rest and enjoyment.'],['Past memories, current thoughts and predictions','Those relate more closely to reflection, not the three daily time categories.']);
q('planning','unequal-periods',5,3,'A student insists that the three recommended daily periods must each last exactly eight hours. What is the best correction?','Their duration should follow needs and priorities; equality is not required','Printed page 60 explicitly says that the three periods need not be equal in length.',
 ['The source requires equal periods but permits shorter days','The text explicitly rejects the premise that equality is necessary.'],['Rest should always be removed before the other periods change','Rest is a legitimate category, not a permanently dispensable one.'],['Only the work period may be planned','The planning principle covers the whole day rather than work alone.']);
q('planning','implementation-sequence',5,3,'Which sequence most closely matches the source’s account of implementation?','Recognize suitable timing, prioritize tasks, and perform them at the appropriate time','Implementation includes judgments about time and priority followed by actual execution.',
 ['Perform the easiest task first and never revise that order','Ease alone is not the page’s criterion of priority or proper timing.'],['Wait for complete certainty about every possible outcome','The source recommends reflection and timely action, not indefinite postponement.'],['Define goals but avoid taking action until the next review','Goal definition alone does not complete implementation.']);
q('planning','haste-delay',4,4,'A person starts a task before its prerequisites are ready, then delays another task that is ready now. Which principle addresses both errors?','Perform each task at its proper time, avoiding premature haste and procrastination','The cited advice criticizes both acting too soon and being lazy about what can be done now.',
 ['Earlier is always better, regardless of prerequisites','The warning against premature haste directly contradicts this rule.'],['Delaying all tasks equally is the fairest schedule','Equal delay does not respect differing readiness and priority.'],['A plan is correct whenever it contains many activities','Quantity of activities does not establish appropriate timing.']);
q('planning','reflection',5,3,'Before choosing a project, a student reviews earlier failures, studies others’ experience and considers future consequences. Which planning principle is most directly illustrated?','Reflection','The page associates reflection with experience, attention to present and future, and thinking ahead about beginning and outcome.',
 ['Verbal gratitude','That concerns expressing thanks rather than examining a prospective plan.'],['Implementation already completed','The student is still evaluating the action rather than performing it.'],['Dividing time into three equal blocks','The scenario concerns reflective evaluation, and equal blocks are not required.']);
q('planning','advice',5,3,'Why can a knowledgeable, experienced adviser who understands the student’s abilities improve a plan?','The adviser can relate experience to the student’s actual capabilities and circumstances','The source values knowledge and experience together with awareness of the person’s abilities.',
 ['Any older person is automatically an expert on every topic','The passage emphasizes relevant knowledge and experience, not universal expertise by age.'],['Advice transfers responsibility for implementation away from the student','Consultation supports a plan but does not perform the student’s responsibilities.'],['Advice makes reflection on consequences unnecessary','Reflection and consultation are complementary planning principles.']);
q('planning','goal-alignment',5,4,'A plan has clear goals and a complete timetable, but most scheduled activities do not advance those goals. Which visible characteristic is most directly missing?','Harmony between the plan’s steps and its goals','The second listed characteristic asks how the plan supports attainment of its goals. Clear goals alone cannot repair irrelevant steps.',
 ['Recognition that time is finite','The stem does not show denial of time’s value; it shows misaligned activities.'],['A larger number of scheduled tasks','Additional irrelevant activities would not solve goal misalignment.'],['Equal allocation to every activity','Equal duration is not the criterion for whether a step advances a goal.']);
q('planning','comprehensive-versus-realistic',5,4,'Plan A omits family responsibilities. Plan B includes everything but requires more time and capacity than the person has. Which paired defects best fit?','A lacks comprehensiveness; B lacks moderation and balance','Comprehensiveness addresses omissions, whereas moderation aligns aspirations with capabilities.',
 ['A lacks moderation; B lacks comprehensiveness','That reverses the specific defects described in the two plans.'],['Both plans fail only because their time blocks are unequal','Unequal blocks are permitted when needs and priorities justify them.'],['Neither fails if the final goals are admirable','Admirable goals do not compensate for omitted duties or an unworkable plan.']);

q('ostentation','independent-means',6,3,'A believer borrows equipment while regarding the lender and equipment as means dependent on God. Does the source’s definition alone make this hidden polytheism?','No; the defining problem is attributing independent efficacy, not merely using a means','The passage permits resorting to others while denying that they independently secure one’s needs.',
 ['Yes; all human assistance is classified as worship','The source distinguishes assistance from independent divine power.'],['Yes; only cash loans are allowed as dependent means','No such cash-versus-equipment distinction appears in the passage.'],['No; human means must therefore be independent of God','That conclusion contradicts the belief explicitly stated in the scenario.']);
q('ostentation','apparent-hidden',6,2,'Which pairing follows the distinction used on printed page 56?','Apparent: worship directed to another being; hidden: assigning independent power while professing one God','The passage contrasts outwardly explicit association with a subtler attribution of independence.',
 ['Apparent: asking for ordinary help; hidden: any private worship','Neither ordinary help nor privacy is the defining criterion in the text.'],['Apparent: silent remembrance; hidden: spoken remembrance','The categories of remembrance are not the categories of polytheism.'],['Apparent: practical gratitude; hidden: verbal gratitude','These gratitude levels are not forms of association in the source.']);
q('ostentation','praise-motive',6,3,'Someone donates specifically so that observers will praise their generosity. What feature makes the example ostentation in this chapter?','The intention to gain recognition through an apparently good deed','The defect is the purpose for which the charitable act is performed, not its merely public location.',
 ['The fact that the recipient receives a real benefit','A recipient’s benefit does not determine the donor’s inward purpose.'],['The mere presence of another person','Public visibility alone does not prove a display-seeking intention.'],['The use of money rather than another gift','The chapter does not make the material form of the gift decisive.']);
q('ostentation','visibility-not-proof',6,4,'Two people perform the same visible act of charity. One seeks praise; the other seeks God’s pleasure. What can be concluded from the shared appearance alone?','It does not establish that their intentions are the same','The chapter distinguishes outwardly correct acts from their motives. The stated intentions, not shared visibility, distinguish these cases.',
 ['Both must be insincere because someone can see them','This confuses being seen with acting in order to be seen.'],['Both must be sincere because the act is useful','Usefulness does not establish purity of purpose.'],['Only private actions can ever count as charity','The passage does not define charity by complete privacy.']);
q('ostentation','worldly-advantage',6,3,'A person prays primarily to impress a supervisor and obtain a raise. Which source example does this most closely match?','Worship used to obtain worldly advantage through display','Printed page 56 explicitly uses favor from a superior and a pay rise to illustrate a non-divine motive.',
 ['Planning the appropriate time for work','The key issue is the motive for worship, not work scheduling.'],['Practical gratitude through service','The described intention is advantage through display rather than grateful use of an ability.'],['Using ordinary causes without any ulterior motive','The stem expressly supplies an ulterior motive.']);
q('ostentation','not-neutral',6,3,'How does the course characterize ostentation when the act looks outwardly correct?','As spiritually harmful rather than merely an absence of extra merit','The text stresses that apparent correctness can coexist with an intention that poisons the deed.',
 ['As automatically harmless if observers approve','Observers’ approval is precisely what may motivate the defective act.'],['As proof that no recipient benefited materially','The moral assessment of motive does not erase possible material benefit.'],['As a defect only in obviously wrongful actions','The chapter focuses on worship and apparently good actions.']);
q('ostentation','subtle-detection',6,3,'Which response best fits the source’s warning that ostentation may be subtle?','Sustained introspection, self-purification and attention to intention','The page calls for inward examination and commitment rather than assuming every motive is immediately obvious.',
 ['Assume a good reputation proves sincerity','A favorable reputation does not expose the motive behind an act.'],['Judge strangers’ motives from photographs alone','The visible act is insufficient to establish inward intention.'],['Stop doing all beneficial acts permanently','The proposed remedy is purification of motive, not abandonment of goodness.']);
q('ostentation','levels-boundary',7,4,'The supplied conclusion says sincerity has levels, while the contents list a separate page on levels of intention. What can this extract establish?','That sincerity has degrees, but not a complete unseen taxonomy of intention','Printed page 58 supports degrees of sincerity; printed page 57 with the listed discussion is not included.',
 ['A precise complete hierarchy can be inferred from the heading alone','A contents heading does not supply the teaching details.'],['The source denies differences in sincerity','The visible conclusion explicitly states that sincerity has levels.'],['Every intention is equally sincere if behavior is identical','The chapter distinguishes motivation from outward behavior.']);

q('sincerity-practice','love',7,2,'Which practice is listed first for establishing sincerity?','Cultivating love of God','The first numbered point presents love as the force that redirects a person’s ambitions toward the beloved.',
 ['Increasing public recognition of one’s worship','That feeds the attention-seeking tendency the section addresses.'],['Treating sincerity as a fixed trait that cannot develop','The entire list consists of practices for developing it.'],['Expecting observers to repay one’s obedience','The final point rejects demanding such human recompense.']);
q('sincerity-practice','impermanence',7,3,'Why does the page recommend reflecting on impermanence when addressing ostentation?','It challenges the value placed on fleeting human status and approval','The practice contrasts fragile, temporary sources of prestige with the enduring divine witness.',
 ['It proves that current actions have no consequences','The chapter emphasizes the continuing moral importance of actions.'],['It removes the need to fulfill duties during life','Reflection should support sincerity in action, not neglect.'],['It establishes that every relationship must be abandoned','The practice critiques ultimate dependence on approval, not all relationships.']);
q('sincerity-practice','concealment',7,3,'A person deliberately avoids advertising a voluntary act of worship. Which recommended practice is being used?','Concealing worship as an exercise against showing off','The fourth point recommends avoiding publicity, even to close associates, as a way to resist ostentation.',
 ['Seeking praise from a smaller and more exclusive audience','Reducing the audience while seeking praise preserves the same motive.'],['Proving that all publicly performed worship is invalid','The exercise does not establish that universal rule.'],['Replacing all obligations with secrecy itself','Privacy is a means of cultivation, not a replacement for duties.']);
q('sincerity-practice','fasting',7,2,'Why is fasting recommended in this particular discussion of sincerity?','It can remain unknown to others unless the person tells them','The fifth point uses fasting as an opportunity for unobtrusive worship rather than public display.',
 ['It automatically guarantees a pure intention in every person','An opportunity for sincerity is not an automatic guarantee.'],['It eliminates the need for any other ethical practice','Fasting is one of seven practices, not a substitute for the others.'],['It requires announcing the fast repeatedly','Repeated announcement would undermine the example’s point.']);
q('sincerity-practice','meaningful-dhikr',7,3,'The recommendation of la ilaha illa Allah combines which two features in the supplied page?','The meaning of divine unity and the possibility of unobtrusive recitation','The sixth point emphasizes its theological meaning and recitation that need not draw others’ attention.',
 ['A guarantee of wealth and exemption from obligations','Neither claim is made by this recommendation.'],['A requirement to attract an audience and perform loudly','That reverses the point about unobtrusive remembrance.'],['A replacement for understanding with sound alone','The text explicitly refers to the enriching content of divine unity.']);
q('sincerity-practice','duty-not-recompense',7,4,'A person says, “I fulfilled my religious duty, so the people who watched owe me praise.” Which practice directly answers this?','Recognize obedience as one’s responsibility without demanding recompense from others','The seventh point states that the benefit of fulfilling duty returns to the person; observers do not thereby owe a reward.',
 ['Conclude that duties matter only when an audience is present','That would reinforce the audience-centered motivation.'],['Replace the duty with a demand for greater recognition','Increasing the demand does not correct the intention.'],['Assume material repayment is the definition of sincerity','The passage defines sincerity through purification of purpose, not repayment.']);
q('sincerity-practice','private-for-later-praise',7,4,'Someone hides a good deed today only so they can tell an impressive story about it tomorrow. What is the most accurate assessment?','Concealment has not by itself purified the praise-seeking intention','Privacy can help cultivate sincerity, but the intended later display preserves the same motive.',
 ['The act must be sincere because nobody saw its beginning','Initial invisibility does not determine the purpose behind the act.'],['Every delayed account of an event is necessarily ostentation','The stem’s stated praise-seeking motive matters; delay alone would not establish it.'],['All seven practices have been completed by temporary concealment','One outward tactic cannot establish completion of the whole inward work.']);
q('sincerity-practice','pairing-remedies',7,4,'Which pair correctly matches a practice to the tendency it addresses?','Reflection on impermanence addresses fixation on transient approval; concealment reduces self-advertising','Both matches follow the second and fourth numbered practices on printed page 58.',
 ['Fasting requires public admiration; concealment guarantees financial success','Neither causal claim is taught in the source.'],['Love of God eliminates action; meaningful remembrance eliminates responsibility','The practices aim to purify action, not remove it.'],['Recognizing harm makes display neutral; fulfilling duty creates a debt from observers','These invert the third and seventh points.']);

q('trust','farmers',8,2,'Why are the farmers who stopped working criticized in the source’s account?','They used reliance on God as an excuse to neglect available work','The story rejects avoidable inactivity presented as trust; responsible effort remains required.',
 ['Farming is presented as incompatible with religious life','The criticism concerns stopping appropriate work, not farming itself.'],['Trust requires complete isolation from natural causes','The same page expressly recommends using natural means.'],['Only public acts count as responsible effort','No public-versus-private criterion explains the story.']);
q('trust','dependent-causes',8,3,'What role do water, food and medicine play in the page’s explanation of trust?','They are usable causes whose capacities are understood as dependent on God','The text combines the use of natural means with denial of their independent power.',
 ['They are all declared ineffective and must be avoided','The page recommends means and gives them as examples of granted capacities.'],['They are independent powers that replace reliance on God','Independence is what the theological explanation denies.'],['Their use guarantees the desired outcome in every case','The passage does not establish an unconditional empirical guarantee.']);
q('trust','camel',13,2,'Which action best expresses the lesson of the camel example?','Secure the camel and trust God','The practical precaution and reliance belong together in the example.',
 ['Leave it unsecured to demonstrate greater reliance','That is the option the example rejects.'],['Secure it while declaring oneself completely independent of God','This keeps the precaution but removes the source’s account of reliance.'],['Wait until it is lost before considering precautions','The lesson concerns taking the available precaution at the proper time.']);
q('trust','serious-effort',13,3,'Which behavior is explicitly one of the five signs of trust on printed page 54?','Serious effort toward the goals of life','Effort is in the list itself, so the text does not treat it as a rival to reliance.',
 ['Refusing every available human means','The chapter permits means while denying their independence.'],['Waiting idly for goals to be achieved','The page’s conclusion explicitly rejects the connection between trust and laziness.'],['Constant comparison with others’ possessions','The list instead emphasizes freedom from greed and fixation on what others have.']);
q('trust','signs-list',13,3,'Which set contains only signs named on the supplied trust page?','Hopeful reliance, dignified confidence, serious effort and peace','These are all listed signs, alongside freedom from greed and preoccupation with sustenance.',
 ['Guaranteed wealth, universal popularity and permanent ease','The page does not make those outcomes signs or guarantees.'],['Neglect of duties, refusal of help and public display','These do not match the listed qualities of trust.'],['Three equal time periods and a complete timetable','Those concern planning, not the five signs of trust.']);
q('trust','confidence-not-isolation',13,4,'A student relies on God while accepting a tutor’s help and studying seriously. Which inference is best supported?','Using appropriate help is compatible with trust when it is not treated as independent power','The trust chapter includes serious effort and dependent means rather than requiring social isolation.',
 ['Accepting help proves that the student has no trust','That would contradict the chapter’s allowance for ordinary means.'],['The tutor must now be regarded as the ultimate independent source','The scenario does not attribute independence to the tutor.'],['Study can stop because help was accepted','Accepting help does not complete all remaining responsibilities.']);
q('trust','umbrella-scope',8,4,'What can the umbrella illustration fairly be used to teach?','Confidence that accords with one’s stated reliance, without a universal weather guarantee','The illustration contrasts professed prayer with an attitude of expectation. It does not establish that every prayer brings immediate rain.',
 ['A rule that taking an umbrella always causes rain','The narrative is not a scientific causal experiment.'],['A promise that every requested event must occur immediately','That goes beyond the example and conflicts with the prayer chapter’s qualification.'],['A requirement to abandon preparation because outcomes are divine','Bringing an umbrella is itself preparation, not abandonment of means.']);
q('trust','degrees-of-trust',13,3,'How does the conclusion describe trust across one’s affairs?','Trust has degrees, including relying on God throughout one’s affairs while continuing to strive','Printed page 54 describes degrees and explicitly retains effort toward perfection.',
 ['Trust has no degrees and is measured only by possessions','Neither the all-or-nothing claim nor the wealth measure matches the conclusion.'],['Greater trust necessarily means less responsibility','The conclusion rejects laziness rather than reducing responsibility.'],['Trust concerns worship only and excludes ordinary life','The conclusion refers to reliance in all affairs.']);
q('trust','source-of-trust',8,3,'What theological conviction is identified as the source of trust in this extract?','Certainty in God and divine unity, including the dependence of causes','The account grounds trust in the conviction that causes receive their capacities from God.',
 ['Certainty that every human plan is infallible','Human plans are not identified as infallible in the source.'],['Belief that effort has no role in attaining goals','The passage explicitly requires effort and means.'],['Proof that all people will approve one’s actions','Human approval is not the foundation of tawakkul.']);
q('trust','hardship-inference',13,4,'A classmate experiences fear during hardship. Why is it unsound to use the list of spiritual signs as a diagnosis that this person lacks faith?','The teaching describes an ethical orientation, not a clinical test of an individual’s condition','The list is not sufficient evidence about all causes of a person’s distress or the state of their faith.',
 ['Because the text says confidence is never relevant','Confidence is listed, but that does not make it a diagnostic instrument.'],['Because hardship is proof that trust is impossible','The chapter discusses trust specifically in relation to difficulty.'],['Because the source guarantees that faithful people face no hardship','No such immunity follows from the list.']);

q('remembrance','apple',9,2,'Why does the special student return without eating the apple?','He finds no place outside God’s awareness','The case illustrates awareness of divine presence even when no other human is watching.',
 ['He could not find a physically private room','The issue is divine awareness, not a shortage of hiding places.'],['He was told that eating apples is prohibited','No general prohibition on apples is part of the story.'],['He was waiting for other students to praise him','Praise-seeking is not the motive reported in the case.']);
q('remembrance','heart',9,2,'Which form of remembrance does the conclusion identify as most important?','Remembrance of the heart','The text gives inward remembrance a central role in enabling benefit from practical and verbal remembrance.',
 ['Unconsidered speech regardless of attention','Speech detached from inward attention is not what the conclusion prioritizes.'],['Observation by other people','Human observation is not a category of remembrance in the passage.'],['The anatomical contraction of the heart','Heart here refers to inward reality and attention, not cardiac physiology.']);
q('remembrance','forms-linked',9,3,'How does remembrance of the heart relate to practical and verbal remembrance in the conclusion?','It prepares a person to benefit from both','The passage links inward attention with meaningful speech and conduct rather than treating them as unrelated.',
 ['It proves that actions never matter','Practical remembrance remains part of the account.'],['It forbids every form of spoken remembrance','Verbal remembrance is retained as something from which one can benefit.'],['It requires replacing all inward awareness with outward sound','That reverses the stated priority.']);
q('remembrance','no-witness',9,4,'A student refuses to cheat even when no classmate or invigilator can see. Which feature connects this scenario to the apple case?','Conduct shaped by awareness beyond visible human observation','The common feature is inward accountability when ordinary observers are absent.',
 ['Certainty that a hidden invigilator is present','The scenario does not depend on an undiscovered human observer.'],['A desire to be praised for avoiding detection','That is different from the inward awareness illustrated.'],['Proof that every quiet person is morally better','The case does not support a universal judgment based on outward quietness.']);
q('remembrance','tranquility-boundary',9,4,'Which use of the chapter’s claim about tranquility stays within a responsible source-based reading?','Describe the spiritual benefit without promising elimination of every anxiety disorder','The text’s spiritual association does not establish a universal clinical outcome for each reader.',
 ['Treat persisting anxiety as proof of insincerity','That diagnoses inward faith from insufficient evidence about distress.'],['Advise everyone to stop clinical care after reciting','The supplied ethical page is not a medical treatment protocol.'],['Claim that remembrance has no practical dimension','The conclusion explicitly retains practical remembrance.']);
q('remembrance','words-and-conduct',9,3,'Someone recites remembered words but gives no attention to their meaning or implications for conduct. Which priority in this page helps identify the gap?','The centrality of inward remembrance that supports practical and verbal expression','The conclusion does not reduce remembrance to sound alone.',
 ['The requirement that all remembrance be publicly visible','The page does not make visibility a requirement.'],['The claim that words automatically replace inward awareness','That reverses the relationship taught in the conclusion.'],['The claim that remembrance is only a planning technique','The topic concerns spiritual awareness and conduct, not timetable design.']);

q('gratitude-levels','sequence',10,2,'Which sequence matches the three levels of thankfulness on printed page 46?','Recognition of the blessing, verbal thanks, practical use of the blessing','The passage moves from appreciation through expression to ethical action.',
 ['Public praise, financial success, comparison with others','Those are not the three levels given.'],['Patience, envy, dissatisfaction','Envy and dissatisfaction are obstacles, not levels of gratitude.'],['Planning, implementation, consultation','Those belong to the planning discussion.']);
q('gratitude-levels','practical-speech',10,3,'A person gives thanks for the ability to speak and then avoids slander while speaking constructively. Which level is most directly shown by the behavior?','Practical thankfulness','The source’s speech example concerns using a blessing in an ethically appropriate way.',
 ['Recognition only','Recognition is involved, but the question asks about the actual use of speech.'],['Verbal thanks only','The behavior goes beyond expressing a phrase of thanks.'],['Ostentation necessarily','The stem gives no intention to gain observers’ praise.']);
q('gratitude-levels','knowledge-service',10,3,'Why is using a learned skill to serve others an example of practical gratitude?','It applies a received ability beneficially rather than merely praising its existence','Printed page 46 explicitly links knowledge and a learned trade with service.',
 ['Because useful action makes all intentions irrelevant','The sincerity material distinguishes usefulness from inward motive.'],['Because possessing knowledge alone completes gratitude','The example emphasizes using knowledge, not possession alone.'],['Because the helper is owed unlimited public praise','Demanding an unlimited reward is not the passage’s meaning.']);
q('gratitude-levels','wealth',10,3,'Which response best matches practical thankfulness for wealth in the supplied list?','Avoid waste and help poor or needy people','The page connects gratitude for wealth with responsible use and assistance.',
 ['Repeat thanks while deliberately wasting resources','Words do not repair misuse of the blessing.'],['Treat wealth as proof of superior moral worth','The later passage warns that possession alone is not spiritual success.'],['Refuse all gratitude to people involved in earning or giving it','Divine gratitude does not cancel obligations to benefactors.']);
q('gratitude-levels','god-no-need',10,3,'Why does the source distinguish thanking God from supplying a need in God?','God is described as needing nothing, so thankfulness benefits the grateful person','The passage treats the capacity and opportunity to thank as another blessing to the person.',
 ['Because thankfulness is therefore pointless','The source presents gratitude as beneficial and important.'],['Because God needs praise as a material resource','That contradicts the stated lack of need.'],['Because practical conduct becomes irrelevant','Practical gratitude is described as the most important stage.']);
q('gratitude-levels','human-benefactor',11,2,'Which response most completely matches the duties toward a human benefactor listed on printed page 47?','Thank them, acknowledge them respectfully, pray for their good and reciprocate when able','The page joins verbal, respectful, spiritual and practical responses to kindness.',
 ['Thank God while denying that any acknowledgment to the person is due','The page explicitly affirms the benefactor’s rights.'],['Repay immediately even if one has no ability, and omit all thanks','The text qualifies repayment by ability and also includes verbal thanks.'],['Offer praise only if it improves one’s public reputation','That substitutes self-interest for sincere gratitude.']);
q('gratitude-levels','when-able',11,4,'Someone cannot yet repay a favor materially. Which course-based response remains appropriate?','Express sincere thanks and goodwill now, and reciprocate when able','The text does not make gratitude disappear when immediate repayment is beyond one’s means.',
 ['Deny the favor until an equal gift can be afforded','Inability to repay does not erase recognition or acknowledgment.'],['Conclude that all gratitude is impossible without money','The listed responses include speech, respect and prayer.'],['Claim the benefactor loses all rights permanently','The passage does not infer this from temporary inability.']);
q('gratitude-levels','words-misuse',10,4,'A person frequently says al-hamdu lillah but repeatedly misuses the ability for which they give thanks. What remains incomplete?','Practical thankfulness','The page distinguishes thanking in words from using the blessing appropriately.',
 ['The ability to pronounce any verbal expression','The stem says verbal thanks are already frequent.'],['A mandatory fourth level called financial repayment to God','The source gives three levels and does not describe such a payment.'],['The requirement that no one ever notice the person','Complete invisibility is not a level of gratitude.']);

q('gratitude-obstacles','five-obstacles',12,2,'Which item is an obstacle to gratitude explicitly listed on printed page 49?','Recognizing a blessing and later forgetting it','The diagram separates forgetting from never recognizing the blessing at all.',
 ['Recognizing the worth of a blessing','Recognition supports gratitude rather than obstructing it.'],['Using knowledge to help others','That is an example of practical thankfulness.'],['Sincere acknowledgment of a benefactor','The preceding page recommends this response.']);
q('gratitude-obstacles','recognition-forgetting',12,4,'A person appreciated a friend’s help at first but later treats it as if it never occurred. Which listed obstacle is most specific?','Forgetting an acknowledged blessing','The initial appreciation distinguishes this case from failure to recognize the blessing in the first place.',
 ['Never recognizing any benefit at any point','That conflicts with the stated initial appreciation.'],['Practical thankfulness','Ignoring the benefit does not apply it or acknowledge it well.'],['Reflection on the value of time','The scenario concerns a forgotten benefit, not scheduling.']);
q('gratitude-obstacles','envy-greed',12,4,'A is preoccupied with gifts another person received. B remains insatiably dissatisfied with their own share. Which pairing best follows the diagram?','A illustrates envy; B illustrates greed or dissatisfaction','The source lists fixation on others’ gifts separately from discontent about what one has.',
 ['A illustrates practical gratitude; B illustrates verbal gratitude','Neither scenario describes an act of gratitude.'],['A illustrates forgetting; B illustrates initial recognition','The stems concern comparison and dissatisfaction, not those distinctions.'],['Both necessarily show that no blessings were ever given','An attitude does not establish absence of benefits.']);
q('gratitude-obstacles','gratitude-for-gratitude',11,3,'Why does the text say that thankfulness itself calls for further thankfulness?','The ability to give thanks is also a blessing','The source treats each expression as enabled by a further gift, encouraging continuing humility.',
 ['A person can become completely independent after enough expressions','The passage emphasizes continued dependence rather than independence.'],['No expression has any value unless it is the final one','The ongoing chain does not cancel the value of present gratitude.'],['Only financial blessings count as gifts','The capacity to thank is itself a non-financial example.']);
q('gratitude-obstacles','prosperity-not-proof',12,4,'A person grows wealthier while misusing resources. Which inference does the chapter explicitly warn against?','Assuming continued abundance proves spiritual success','Printed pages 47 and 49 distinguish possession of blessings from their grateful and ethical use.',
 ['Recognizing that blessings can be misused','That recognition is part of the warning itself.'],['Distinguishing gratitude from mere possession','The chapter requires this distinction.'],['Considering how resources are actually used','Use of resources is central to practical gratitude.']);
q('gratitude-obstacles','patience-gratitude',12,2,'Which pairing expresses the two responses contrasted on printed page 49?','Patience in difficulty and gratitude for blessings','The passage presents these as complementary responses in the life of faith.',
 ['Envy in difficulty and pride in abundance','Those responses do not match the recommended ethical qualities.'],['Idleness in difficulty and waste in abundance','The source’s planning and gratitude teaching rejects these patterns.'],['Silence in every circumstance and avoidance of all action','The passage includes practical gratitude, not universal inaction.']);
q('gratitude-obstacles','increase-not-contract',11,4,'Which interpretation of increased blessings best avoids adding an unsupported mechanical rule?','It is a theological teaching about gratitude, not a fixed formula for immediate financial return','The passage does not specify a monetary rate, deadline or universal material outcome for each expression of thanks.',
 ['Each spoken thank-you guarantees a set amount of money tomorrow','No such amount or timetable appears in the source.'],['All poor people are therefore ungrateful','That diagnoses character from circumstances without support.'],['Thankfulness is unnecessary unless it produces visible wealth','The text treats gratitude as an obligation and spiritual benefit beyond such a condition.']);
q('gratitude-obstacles','humility-chain',11,4,'A student concludes, “Because perfect repayment of all blessings is impossible, I should stop giving thanks.” What is the best correction?','The continuing debt motivates ongoing gratitude rather than making present gratitude pointless','The text’s chain of gratitude-for-gratitude supports humility and continuity, not abandonment.',
 ['Only an act that finishes every obligation can have value','The passage does not impose that all-or-nothing requirement.'],['One verbal phrase establishes permanent self-sufficiency','That contradicts the continuing dependence described.'],['Gratitude should be replaced by envy of those with more','Envy is expressly listed as an obstacle.']);

q('prayer','effort',14,3,'A person asks for an objective but refuses every reasonable step toward it. Which point on the prayer page most directly challenges this?','Supplication accompanies striving, and lack of effort can be relevant to non-attainment','The page says the person beseeches God and strives, and later names absent effort as one possible explanation.',
 ['Every prayer must produce the exact outcome without any action','The passage explicitly qualifies this expectation.'],['Ordinary material means can never be part of a response','The source allows material as well as extraordinary means.'],['Prayer is defined only as verbal gratitude','The page describes presenting needs and relying on God.']);
q('prayer','five-effects',14,2,'Which is among the five effects of prayer listed in the extract?','Remembrance of God and proximity to Him','This is the first listed effect, together with His love.',
 ['Guaranteed superiority over every classmate','The page does not teach this competitive guarantee.'],['Exemption from all effort','The account expressly retains striving.'],['Automatic possession of any requested object','The response is qualified by wisdom and benefit.']);
q('prayer','wise-response',14,4,'A requested outcome does not occur. Which conclusion is compatible with the page’s account without pretending to know the particular reason?','A wise response need not be identical to the requested outcome','The page offers possible explanations and distinguishes a response from receiving exactly what was requested.',
 ['The requester must have committed a specific hidden sin','This asserts a particular cause without evidence and ignores other possibilities.'],['God must have failed to hear the request','That is not the conclusion drawn within the source’s framework.'],['Every request is therefore meaningless','The text assigns prayer significance even when the specific object is withheld.']);
q('prayer','knife-analogy',14,3,'What is the visible mother-and-knife analogy intended to illustrate?','Withholding a desired object can be consistent with care for the requester','The analogy contrasts the child’s desire with a caregiver’s awareness of danger.',
 ['All requests should be granted if repeated insistently','The mother’s refusal contradicts that interpretation.'],['A refusal always proves hostility','The example specifically allows refusal motivated by care.'],['The exact continuation after the scanned page is known','The scan ends before the example is completed.']);
q('prayer','means-response',14,3,'Through which means does the page say a response to prayer may occur?','Material or supernatural means, according to divine wisdom','The source does not restrict a response to the suspension of ordinary causes.',
 ['Only a visible miracle','That excludes the explicitly mentioned material means.'],['Only money transferred immediately','Neither money nor immediacy is required by the statement.'],['Only through complete abandonment of action','The passage continues to emphasize effort.']);
q('prayer','knowledge-link',14,3,'How does knowledge of God motivate supplication in the page’s argument?','God is understood as the source of perfection and benefits, so needs are directed to Him','The argument links divine sufficiency and generosity with dependence and prayer.',
 ['God is described as learning needs only after being told','That is not the theological premise used on the page.'],['The person becomes independent of all sources of benefit','The account emphasizes dependence rather than self-sufficiency.'],['Supplication is needed only to gain human status','That substitutes the motive criticized in the ostentation chapter.']);
q('prayer','effects-versus-method',14,4,'Which option contains only effects named under “Why should we pray?”, rather than a planning method or gratitude level?','Protection, rescue from enemies, increased sustenance and purification from sins','All four belong to the five-effect list; remembrance/proximity/love is the remaining grouped effect.',
 ['Consultation, prioritization, implementation and reflection','These belong to planning, not this prayer-effects list.'],['Recognition, verbal thanks and practical gratitude','These are the gratitude levels from printed page 46.'],['Concealment, fasting and reflection on impermanence','These appear as practices for sincerity.']);
q('prayer','nonattainment-hope',14,4,'Why can the believer’s hope remain when the particular objective is not attained, according to the supplied argument?','Divine goodness and wisdom are not reduced to fulfillment of that one desire','The paragraph explicitly connects continued hope with trust in goodness even without attainment.',
 ['The person must deny that any effort was required','The same paragraph joins prayer with striving.'],['Every non-attainment proves the person secretly wanted failure','The source supplies no basis for that psychological claim.'],['All stated objectives are irrelevant to practical life','The text allows goals and effort while qualifying expectations.']);

q('repentance','two-reasons',15,3,'Which pair best captures the page’s two arguments against postponing repentance?','Wrongdoing can become entrenched, and future time before death is uncertain','The source connects repetition with habit and also warns that another opportunity is not guaranteed.',
 ['All habits are unchangeable, and everyone knows their exact time of death','Both claims contradict the page’s reason for beginning correction now.'],['Only public mistakes matter, and private acts leave no effect','The page’s concern includes the person’s inward disposition.'],['Good conduct must be dramatic, and small acts are worthless','The discussion explicitly values small but sustained actions.']);
q('repentance','habit',15,3,'What is the proposed link between repeated wrongdoing and harder later correction?','Repetition can form habits that become rooted in conduct and disposition','The discussion describes a process of habituation rather than a single isolated event with no continuing effect.',
 ['Each repetition automatically weakens the tendency','The page warns of reinforcement, not automatic extinction.'],['The act’s consequences disappear if nobody notices','The argument does not depend on human observation.'],['Correction during life is declared impossible after one mistake','The text urges prompt repentance rather than announcing impossibility.']);
q('repentance','small-sustained',15,3,'Why does the discussion value a small continuing good action over a large act that is not sustained?','Repeated good action can form a stable constructive habit','The explanation connects continuity with the formation and strengthening of disposition.',
 ['Magnitude never matters in any ethical judgment','The comparison does not establish that unrestricted rule.'],['A dramatic act always erases every future responsibility','No such exemption appears in the passage.'],['Continuity is relevant only to bad habits','The discussion explicitly includes righteous and ignoble patterns.']);
q('repentance','fruit',15,3,'What does fruit ripening while attached to its tree illustrate in the cited account?','The opportunity for moral development while earthly life continues','Detachment marks the end of the analogy’s period of growth; the focus is opportunity for change.',
 ['A literal biological description of the human soul','The comparison is an explanatory metaphor, not a biological identity.'],['A guarantee that postponement improves repentance','The urgency of repentance is the opposite of that inference.'],['A claim that all people become good without choices','The account connects development with worship, devotion and choices.']);
q('repentance','consequences-versus-change',15,4,'Why does the cited passage distinguish regret after seeing inevitable consequences from repentance during life?','It contrasts compelled reaction with an inward moral transformation while choice and development remain possible','The explanation asks what motivates the apparent change, not merely whether regret is felt.',
 ['Any strong emotion is automatically complete moral transformation','The distinction specifically rejects reducing transformation to reaction.'],['People must wait for consequences before beginning correction','The chapter urges repentance at the first opportunity.'],['Earthly choices are said to have no moral role','The whole account locates development in choices during life.']);
q('repentance','moral-metaphor',15,4,'How should the page’s comparison of sin to disease and repentance to medicine be used?','As a moral analogy for harm and correction, not as a clinical diagnosis','The comparison concerns the soul and ethical development; it is not a medical treatment protocol.',
 ['To diagnose an illness from a person’s moral history','The page provides no clinical basis for that diagnosis.'],['To replace medical treatment with an ethical label','A moral analogy does not establish a replacement treatment.'],['To claim that every bodily disease proves wrongdoing','That conclusion is not supported by the passage.']);
q('repentance','uncertain-opportunity',15,3,'A person is confident they will repent after graduation. Which independent concern remains even if habits were not becoming stronger?','They do not know whether that future opportunity will exist','Uncertainty about remaining life is a second reason, distinct from habituation, against delay.',
 ['Their future opportunity is guaranteed by making a promise','A present intention does not guarantee future time.'],['Repentance is only permitted after graduation','No such timing restriction appears in the source.'],['The text says planning future action is always wrong','The criticism concerns postponing correction, not all planning.']);
q('repentance','death-boundary',15,3,'Which timing rule follows the supplied page’s argument for immediate repentance?','Begin at the first opportunity in life; do not rely on repentance at death or afterward','The page places repentance in earthly life and states that the door closes at death and afterward; uncertainty about remaining time makes delay unsafe.',
 ['It is best to postpone until consequences become inevitable at death','The cited case distinguishes that compelled regret from inward moral transformation.'],['Only a young person has any opportunity for correction during life','The warning especially addresses youth but does not impose that age-only rule.'],['A promise to repent guarantees that enough life remains','The page explicitly denies certainty about even the next hour or minute.']);
q('repentance','difficult-not-hopeless',15,4,'A student says, “My repeated wrong action has become habitual, so there is no point starting to change.” Which response best follows the page?','Entrenchment makes prompt correction more urgent; it does not prove that change during life is impossible','The passage warns that correction becomes more difficult and uses that warning to motivate immediate action.',
 ['Difficulty and impossibility are interchangeable','A harder process is not thereby an impossible one.'],['Waiting always makes a harmful habit easier to change','That reverses the mechanism described in the passage.'],['Only an enormous one-time act could matter','The discussion also emphasizes small sustained good actions.']);
